    "title": "How it works",
    "hook": "Indian businesses rarely sell at one price. Most platforms assume otherwise.",
    "script": "",
    "caption": "Retail rate. Wholesale rate. Slab pricing above 50 units. A rate you negotiated with one buyer two years ago and still honour. Minimum order quantities that differ by product.\n\nEvery one of those is normal, and every one of them is awkward on a platform built around a single price field.\n\n**What proper pricing support looks like:**\n→ Customer groups with their own rate cards\n→ Quantity slabs that change the unit price mid-cart\n→ Per-customer overrides that survive a catalogue update\n→ MOQ enforced at the cart, not apologised for at checkout\n\nIf you've been keeping wholesale off your website because \"the site can't do it\" — that's a build decision somebody made, not a law.",
    "hashtags": "#EcommerceWebsite #D2C #CustomSoftware #Dehradun #SmallBusinessIndia",
    "imagePrompt": "Blueprint schematic on warm paper of a stepped pricing curve with four horizontal tiers and thin orange annotation lines marking the breakpoints, technical drafting style, measurement marks, faint grid. soft directional daylight from the left, warm paper #ECEAE4 background, one vivid orange #F4501A accent and nothing else saturated, editorial product photography, shot on 50mm, shallow depth of field, generous empty space in the upper third for a text overlay, muted natural palette, photorealistic, 4:5 aspect ratio. no text, no words, no letters, no numbers, no logos, no watermarks, no people looking at camera, no stock-photo handshakes, no clutter, not oversaturated, no HDR, no lens flare.",
